WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.101
And educators across the

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.602
country are raising the alarm

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.371
over a new technology

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:06.706
that is making it easier to cheat.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.175
It's called Chat G p t.

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:10.010
It's a chat

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.779
bot that runs on artificial intelligence,

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:14.147
and it can do

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:15.682
pretty much any things

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:16.416
you tell it to do

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:17.817
from solving a complex

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:18.918
math problem

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:21.955
to writing essays on nearly any topic.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:23.556
So Chat

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:26.259
T has basically exploded in popularity

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:27.494
in just a matter of weeks

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.029
since it was released,

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.497
and teachers are now saying

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:32.599
that it's not all that easy to catch.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.434
And that includes our next guest.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.869
Darren Hick is an assistant

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:36.736
professor

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.139
of philosophy at Furman University.

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:41.741
He is also an expert in plagiarism

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:42.776
and copyright law.

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:44.778
And he just wrote a lengthy post warning

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.413
that this new technology

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.082
is going to spark a flood of cheating.

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:52.285
So, Darren, you're concerned,

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:54.354
but you were actually one

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:56.056
who recently busted a student

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:59.159
for using this chat bot to write an essay

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.361
if you were able to catch it.

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:02.762
How hard could it really be

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:03.663
to figure out if it's

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:04.931
a bot or a real person?

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.867
Depends on the level of material

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:08.768
that we're talking about.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:09.602
In my case,

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.871
it was an upper level philosophy class,

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.574
and GPT

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.010
doesn't have the sophistication

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.312
to deal with some some obscure topics.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.681
But had it been an intro class,

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:22.082
you know,

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.283
writing an essay

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.784
to show that you understood

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.686
some basic fundamental issues

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:30.290
and theories, it would have been harder

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:33.359
And so that's one student how,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.427
you know, if you're a teacher

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:35.795
or you're teaching a

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:37.897
maybe an intro college level course,

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.266
how can people even prove

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.136
that it was a bot that wrote the essay?

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.872
Yeah, that's the hard part.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:46.773
I have,

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:47.073
you know,

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:47.674
the instincts

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:48.842
that I've built over the past

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.844
15 years of teaching

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.312
that says this paper wasn't

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.647
written by this student, and that's fine.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.516
And in more traditional forms

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.918
of plagiarism, cheating off the Internet

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.120
copy pasting stuff,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.255
I can go in to find additional

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.157
proof evidence that I can then

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.992
bring into a board hearing,

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.227
something like that.

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.562
In this case, there's nothing out there

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:10.530
that I can point to

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.632
and say, here is the material they took.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.069
It's really a new form of an old problem

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.438
where students would pay somebody

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:20.106
or get somebody to write their paper

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:20.607
for them.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:21.207
Say An essay

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.209
farmer friend has taken a course before.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.444
This is like that.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:27.013
Only it's instantaneous and free

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.649
and it's on the Internet.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.783
And they can just do it

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.752
basically whenever they want.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.220
The other crazy thing about this

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.724
is that this technology basically learns

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:40.193
the more people use it, the smarter

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:43.196
it gets, the more sophisticated it gets.

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:45.899
Is there any going back from this?

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:46.799
Is this going to

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.501
dramatically just change

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.703
the future of homework or really

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:52.405
any other kind of work

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.473
that requires humans

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:56.176
to write things or to communicate?

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:58.211
It's I mean, it's a game changer

00:02:58.211 --> 00:02:59.946
and it's just the first of its kind.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:01.080
And as you point out,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.582
this thing is instant

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.783
and it's trained to learn.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:04.417
So it's

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.753
trainers will be working on this thing

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:07.487
a month from now.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:08.922
It's going to be smarter than it is

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:09.389
today.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:10.356
And a year from now,

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.091
it's going to be so much smarter.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.160
And those things that made it stand out

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.962
when it wrote an essay for an upper level

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.363
philosophy class

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:18.298
aren't going to stand out

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.732
as easily anymore.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.868
I think there's really two things that

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.704
teachers need to think about strategies

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:27.607
for how they're going to make assignments

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:30.343
that aren't easily written with

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:31.978
or easily detected,

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:33.980
at least when it's turned in that way.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:35.648
But I think the bigger issue

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:36.950
is going to be administrations

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.951
who have to figure out

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.118
how they're going to adjudicate

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.119
these kind of cases.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:43.523
When I bring evidence to a board

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.291
or a disciplinary committee,

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.659
what evidence is enough?

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.628
What's the right sort of evidence

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:49.829
to be putting forward?

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:51.464
And this I mean, it's brand new.

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:52.532
We weren't ready for this.

